996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157395 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1045 "Moments of Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157533 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "55c2127a-d43f-53c5-907e-c8d12316517c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1061 39753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dc1ae12-0989-5596-8114-7d74427919d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158291 #edb/ref 1061 39754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "897313ee-485e-51e5-8fec-b665d482b874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1061 39755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08325c1e-fe0c-5a7b-b6e5-8f27c2951461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1061 39756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bce87273-1a01-58a6-825f-736ef62ef788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1061 39757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fb3611b-a9ba-58f4-9159-00a7c9b29393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X,$ respectively. \n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ the expected value of the the random variable $X^2,$ we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1065 6591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1064 "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X.$\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1065 6592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1067 "Higher Moments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427291 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427291 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427291 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33cb4776-7d73-5e89-be66-3abb80c22cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427301 #edb/ref 1080 "1-\\frac{1}{e}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427301 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427301 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d5bf664-a18f-566d-a4f7-3bd738b55f2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427309 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427309 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17